WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.667
Laurie found a place to stay in Florence, and for the next few days, he and Amy were inseparable.

2
00:00:07.668 --> 00:00:13.794
Amy noticed some changes in Laurie: for example, he wasn't throwing money around like before,

3
00:00:13.795 --> 00:00:19.884
he showed more consideration for others, and he seemed to be more responsible, more mature.

4
00:00:19.885 --> 00:00:23.329
Amy mentioned what she had noticed to him.

5
00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:28.553
"After what you told me in Rome, I did a lot of soul-searching," he admitted.

6
00:00:28.554 --> 00:00:34.624
"I'm sorry if I was blunt," Amy began apologetically but Laurie stopped her.

7
00:00:34.625 --> 00:00:38.961
"No, I should be thanking you," he said. "You were right.

8
00:00:38.962 --> 00:00:42.464
I was too engrossed in myself to realize it then."

9
00:00:42.465 --> 00:00:48.384
"Well, if you feel that way, then I'm glad I spoke up," Amy said with a smile.

10
00:00:48.385 --> 00:00:54.190
"After Beth died," Laurie continued in a softer voice, "everything changed.

11
00:00:54.191 --> 00:00:59.402
I didn't want to waste another moment on anything insignificant or meaningless.

12
00:00:59.403 --> 00:01:03.424
I need purpose and direction to guide me through this life."

13
00:01:03.425 --> 00:01:11.461
Laughing, Amy laid a hand on Laurie's and said, "Oh no! I think I've made a philosopher out of you."

14
00:01:11.462 --> 00:01:19.019
Taking her hand, Laurie looked into her eyes and said, "Not a philosopher but a real person."

15
00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:29.076
They sat there like that for a while, looking into each others' eyes, both of them realizing that their friendship had turned into something deeper.

16
00:01:29.077 --> 00:01:36.892
Back home, loneliness was a word and a state that Jo had long forgotten as she threw herself into Mr. Laurence's project.

17
00:01:36.893 --> 00:01:42.696
One day she and Mr. Laurence were visiting possible sites to build the school on.

18
00:01:42.697 --> 00:01:46.649
"What is the school going to be called, by the way?" Jo asked.

19
00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:51.195
"I thought I'd leave that up to you," Mr. Laurence replied.

20
00:01:51.196 --> 00:01:56.507
Grateful for his thoughtfulness, Jo promised she would think about it and let him know.

21
00:01:56.508 --> 00:02:02.913
"The school will be small, but I want the students to enjoy all areas of education:

22
00:02:02.914 --> 00:02:08.822
not just academic, but arts and sports as well," Mr. Laurence explained.

23
00:02:08.823 --> 00:02:16.204
"It sounds like just the kind of place Beth would have loved," Jo said warmly.

24
00:02:16.205 --> 00:02:24.017
They were standing in front of a large house that looked deserted, yet it was appealing to both Jo and Mr. Laurence at once.

25
00:02:24.018 --> 00:02:29.845
"For starters, the roof needs repairing, and the exterior will need to be repainted,"

26
00:02:29.846 --> 00:02:36.780
Mr. Laurence remarked, gently tugging his chin in deep thought. "What do you think, Jo?"

27
00:02:36.781 --> 00:02:42.389
Jo placed her hands on her hips and made as professional a judgment as an amateur can make.

28
00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:47.140
She nodded agreeably and said, "It's perfect."

29
00:02:47.141 --> 00:02:52.388
Across the ocean, Laurie and Amy were also finding many things they agreed upon.

30
00:02:52.389 --> 00:02:58.377
From Florence they agreed, with the consent of Aunt March, of course, to travel next to Venice.

31
00:02:58.378 --> 00:03:06.628
Amy filled two sketchbooks in the first three days they were there, every street being more colorful and exotic than the one before.

32
00:03:06.629 --> 00:03:12.669
On the fourth day, Laurie treated Amy to a ride on a gondola that he steered himself.

33
00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:19.454
As they glided over the water, Amy sketched the unique architecture while Laurie watched her with a smile.

34
00:03:19.455 --> 00:03:24.341
"Stop staring at me. You're making me nervous," she teased.

35
00:03:24.342 --> 00:03:29.468
"I can't help it. You're fascinating to watch," he said in return.

36
00:03:29.469 --> 00:03:35.684
Laurie steered the gondola toward a small bridge that led to a beautiful flower garden.

37
00:03:35.692 --> 00:03:42.792
"I want to show you this breathtaking spot," Laurie said as he stood up and pointed toward the garden with his oar.

38
00:03:42.793 --> 00:03:48.068
No one really knew how it happened, but Laurie lost his balance.

39
00:03:48.069 --> 00:03:55.825
For a second, he looked like a tightrope walker at the circus, wiggling this way and that to stay standing.

40
00:03:55.826 --> 00:04:01.910
But in the end, the weight of the oar won, and down he went into the water.

41
00:04:01.911 --> 00:04:04.984
"Laurie!" Amy shouted.

42
00:04:04.985 --> 00:04:09.313
Laurie shook his wet hair as he pulled himself into the gondola.

43
00:04:09.314 --> 00:04:13.737
"My ego is bruised, but I'm okay. Where's the oar?"

44
00:04:13.738 --> 00:04:17.367
Laurie looked around and saw it floating gently away.

45
00:04:17.368 --> 00:04:21.324
"We have to get that back!" he said, panicking.

46
00:04:21.325 --> 00:04:25.383
There was nothing in the boat to use except Amy's sketchbook.

47
00:04:25.384 --> 00:04:31.606
Without a second thought, Amy rolled it up and used it to paddle toward the runaway oar.

48
00:04:31.607 --> 00:04:39.815
"There you go," she said, snatching the oar out of the water when they reached it and shaking out her dripping, ruined sketchbook.

49
00:04:39.816 --> 00:04:45.202
Impressed beyond words, Laurie stared at Amy with a strange look.

50
00:04:45.203 --> 00:04:49.102
Then he simply said, "We make a great team, you and I.

51
00:04:49.103 --> 00:04:52.424
Do you think we can do it forever?"

52
00:04:52.425 --> 00:05:00.386
Understanding what his words implied, Amy smiled shyly and said, "Yes, I think we can."

53
00:05:00.387 --> 00:05:10.248
Jo was unaware of the tremendous development that had just happened in Venice, but she was also about to embark on a life-altering course.

54
00:05:10.249 --> 00:05:16.508
She had finally completed her novel, the one she had recorded in the special notebook from the Professor.

55
00:05:16.509 --> 00:05:23.069
With mixed feelings, yet determined to move ahead with her goal, Jo put the notebook into an envelope,

56
00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:28.894
wrote a New York address on it, took it to the post office, and mailed it off.

57
00:05:28.895 --> 00:05:35.063
While Jo was in New York, the Professor had helped her get the addresses of several publishers.

58
00:05:35.064 --> 00:05:41.343
Until recently, Jo had been content to sell her short stories to local newspapers and magazines,

59
00:05:41.344 --> 00:05:45.450
because she was reluctant to reach out to a bigger wider market.

60
00:05:45.451 --> 00:05:50.177
After her sister died, however, Jo felt less afraid of failure.

61
00:05:50.178 --> 00:05:55.101
She decided to take chances and to set her standards higher.

62
00:05:55.102 --> 00:06:05.476
Having faced so many obstacles in life, Jo now had the confidence that even if she did fail, she could get right back on her feet again.

63
00:06:05.477 --> 00:06:13.178
A few days later, two urgent telegrams were delivered: one to the Laurence household and the other to the March family.

64
00:06:13.179 --> 00:06:19.685
Jo happened to be the one who received it at their home, so she read it aloud for her parents to hear.

65
00:06:19.686 --> 00:06:22.951
"It's from our Amy," Jo said cheerfully.

66
00:06:22.952 --> 00:06:30.598
"It reads, 'Dear Mother, Father, and my darling sisters: I am delighted to tell you that I am engaged to . . .'"

67
00:06:30.599 --> 00:06:34.731
Jo paused as she read the name, "'Laurie.'"

68
00:06:34.732 --> 00:06:40.037
"Jo, are you all right?" Mother said as she saw her daughter's white face.

69
00:06:40.038 --> 00:06:45.366
"Yes, I'm just shocked. I . . . I never thought . . . ," Jo stammered.

70
00:06:45.367 --> 00:06:51.515
"I need some fresh air." She staggered out the door and walked briskly to the pond.

71
00:06:51.516 --> 00:07:00.551
Jo wasn't upset that Laurie had found someone else to love after she rejected him, but that telegram signaled another change in their relationship.

72
00:07:00.552 --> 00:07:05.033
For Jo, this was going to take a little time to accept.

73
00:07:05.034 --> 00:07:11.106
Sitting alone looking out at the pond, Jo was reminded of Beth's words on their last trip.

74
00:07:11.107 --> 00:07:19.314
She heard Beth's voice say, "If we spent our time concentrating on one sight, we might miss out on the others.

75
00:07:19.315 --> 00:07:24.946
I'd rather see everything once than see the same scene over and over again.

76
00:07:24.947 --> 00:07:34.374
Besides, the picture we take with our heart is always so much more precious than the ones we capture with a brush, don't you think?"

77
00:07:34.375 --> 00:07:38.899
"You're right, Beth," Jo said, looking up toward the sky.

78
00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:43.831
"Life is constantly changing, and so are relationships.

79
00:07:43.832 --> 00:07:49.588
We can't expect things to remain the same when we ourselves are forever evolving.

80
00:07:49.589 --> 00:07:59.261
But we will always have the precious memories of the past to look back on with fondness."

